          "filename": "Cabman's Shelter, Embankment Place - geograph.org.uk - 608350.jpg",
          "caption": "Cabman's Shelter, Embankment Place. In 1874 the Earl of Shaftesbury started a charity to provide shelters for Cabmen, where they could get a hot meal while working. They were not allowed to leave their cabs, so they had to be situated by (or in) the road. 13 still exist (of 61 built between 1875 &amp; 1914) and all are Grade II listed buildings.\n\nBehind it on the left is The Playhouse Theatre, with Charing Cross Station on the right.",
          "alt": "Cabman's Shelter, Embankment Place. In 1874 the Earl of Shaftesbury started a charity to provide shelters for Cabmen, where they could get a hot meal while working. They were not allowed to leave their cabs, so they had to be situated by (or in) the road. 13 still exist (of 61 built between 1875 &amp; 1914) and all are Grade II listed buildings.\n\nBehind it on the left is The Playhouse Theatre, with Charing Cross Station on the right.",
